   "text": {
    "L1": "Heat moves from a hotter object to a colder object.",
    "L2": "A metal may feel colder than wood in the same room, but that does not mean it is colder.",
    "L3": "Metal conducts heat away from your hand faster, so your hand loses heat quickly.",
    "L4": "In a solid, heat is passed from particle to particle, from the hot end towards the cold end.",
    "L5": "The particles pass heat on, but the material itself stays in place.",
    "L6": "Metals such as steel, aluminium, copper and iron are good conductors of heat.",
    "L7": "Wood, plastic, clay, cloth, wool and air are poor conductors, so they slow heat flow.",
    "L8": "That is why a metal cooking pot can have a wood or plastic handle.",
    "L9": "Remember the direction: heat travels from hot to cold until both reach the same temperature."
   },
